  "statements": {
    "uniform-circular-motion": "Uniform circular motion is motion in a circular path at constant speed, and it is the simplest form of curved motion. The centripetal acceleration, the centripetal force and the orbits of satellites are all worked out for this case first.",
    "acceleration-in-multiples-of-g": "Dividing a centripetal acceleration by the acceleration due to gravity states it in multiples of g, which tells you at once how it compares with what we feel standing still. Centrifuges carry ratings in multiples of g, astronauts' tolerance to launch and reentry is stated the same way, and the ratio shows whether a ride would be uncomfortable or dangerous.",
    "kepler-third-law-derivation": "Substituting the average speed $v = 2\\pi r/T$ into $GM/r = v^2$ gives $T^2 = 4\\pi^2 r^3/GM$, and taking the ratio of that expression for two satellites of the same parent body gives Kepler's third law, with $M$ cancelling. The derivation shows that the force of gravity is the cause of what Kepler only described, and why the third law compares satellites of one parent body only.",
    "descriptive-versus-causal-law": "Kepler's laws describe how the planets move; Newton's universal law of gravitation says why, and that single equation covers every situation in which gravity acts. The earlier Ptolemaic model was a list of facts, with a different rule for each heavenly body and no cause behind any of it, while the Copernican model needs a small set of rules and a single underlying force."
  },
